# ---------- 1. Introduction ----------
D.h1('1. Introduction')
D.para("Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), now also described as metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD), is the most common chronic liver condition worldwide, affecting roughly a quarter to a third of adults.{younossi16,younossi23,riazi22} It encompasses a spectrum from simple steatosis to steatohepatitis, fibrosis and cirrhosis, and it is defined by hepatic fat accumulation in the absence of other causes of liver disease.{chalasani18,eslam20,rinella23} Its pathogenesis is closely tied to obesity, insulin resistance and dyslipidaemia, and intrahepatic fat, more than visceral fat, tracks the metabolic complications of obesity.{fabbrini10,fabbrini09} Despite this burden, the causal contribution of individual circulating lipid fractions to liver fat remains poorly defined.")
D.para("Remnant cholesterol (RC) is the cholesterol content of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins, that is, very low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) and intermediate-density lipoprotein remnants in the fasting state, together with chylomicron remnants after a meal.{nordvarbo14,nord16} Because these particles are not removed efficiently by the LDL receptor, they can enter the arterial intima, and RC has emerged as a cardiovascular risk factor that is partly independent of LDL cholesterol and apolipoprotein B.{quispe21,duran20} Genetic and observational evidence together support a causal role of RC in ischaemic heart disease.{varbo13,richardson20} The liver is the origin of VLDL. Fatty acids reaching the liver from adipose tissue, de novo lipogenesis and the diet supply both the triglyceride stored in hepatocytes and the triglyceride secreted in VLDL,{donnelly05} so RC and hepatic fat share a metabolic source and are plausibly linked in both directions.")
D.para("In observational studies, higher RC has been repeatedly associated with prevalent and incident NAFLD, including in non-obese people and in analyses of cumulative exposure,{zou21,miao23,liu24} and with insulin resistance among patients with metabolic dysfunction-associated fatty liver disease.{wang24} Such associations are vulnerable to confounding and reverse causation. Adiposity, insulin resistance, diet, alcohol use and physical inactivity influence both RC and liver fat, while hepatic steatosis itself increases the secretion of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins. Conventional analyses can therefore not show whether elevated RC contributes to fatty liver or is mainly one of its consequences.")
D.para("The genetic architecture of NAFLD adds a further complication. Variants in PNPLA3, TM6SF2, GCKR and TRIB1 influence hepatic lipid handling and are associated with liver fat.{romeo08,kozlitina14,speliotes11,fairfield22} Some of these loci also change circulating lipoproteins, and not always in the direction one would expect. The TM6SF2 variant that increases liver fat, for example, lowers circulating lipids because it impairs the secretion of triglyceride-rich particles from the liver.{kozlitina14} Genetic instruments for RC are therefore likely to include variants that act on the liver itself, which makes a Mendelian randomization (MR) analysis of this particular exposure and outcome pair unusually exposed to horizontal pleiotropy and calls for explicit outlier handling.")
D.para("MR uses genetic variants as instrumental variables to estimate the causal effect of an exposure on an outcome, and it is less prone to confounding and reverse causation because alleles are allocated at conception.{ds03,ds14,lawlor08,sanderson22} Two MR studies have addressed RC and fatty liver with conflicting results. A two-sample MR of RC with many cardiometabolic outcomes found no causal association with NAFLD.{guan23} A one-sample MR in the Taiwan Biobank reported very large positive effects on NAFLD and related definitions, but exposure and outcome came from the same cohort and a single ancestry.{ko25} Because MR findings need robust support and transparent reporting,{burgess24,skriv21} we investigated the causal effect of genetically predicted RC on NAFLD using three large NAFLD GWAS, a battery of pleiotropy-robust estimators, explicit outlier and influence diagnostics, a positive control analysis, and a formal assessment of statistical power and sample overlap. Our aim was to determine whether RC is causally related to NAFLD and to state which effect sizes the available data can and cannot exclude.")
# ---------- 2. Method ----------
D.h1('2. Method')
D.h2('2.1. Study design and data sources')
D.para("This study used a two-sample Mendelian randomization (TSMR) design based on GWAS summary-level data to explore the potential causal link between RC (as the exposure) and NAFLD (as the outcome) in individuals of European ancestry (Fig. 1). The analysis relies on three core assumptions: the genetic instruments are associated with RC (relevance), they are independent of confounders of the RC–NAFLD relation (independence), and they affect NAFLD only through RC (exclusion restriction). The study adhered to the STROBE-MR reporting guideline{skriv21,skrivbmj21} (checklist in Supplemental Digital Content 1) and followed current guidance for MR investigations.{burgess20g} The analysis was not pre-registered.")
D.para("For the exposure variable, we used summary statistics for RC from the GWAS of 249 NMR metabolic traits by Richardson et al,{richardson22} measured on the Nightingale Health platform in up to 115,082 participants of European ancestry from the UK Biobank.{sudlow15,bycroft18,soininen15,wurtz17} RC corresponds to the non-HDL, non-LDL cholesterol fraction quantified by the platform. Values were inverse rank-normalised, so effect sizes are expressed per 1-SD higher RC, and associations were estimated with BOLT-LMM adjusted for age, sex, fasting time and genotyping array.{loh15} Data on the outcome, NAFLD, were taken from three European-ancestry GWAS (Table 1): the electronic-health-record meta-analysis by Ghodsian et al (GWAS Catalog GCST90091033), which combined eMERGE, FinnGen (data freeze 4), UK Biobank and Estonian Biobank and included 8,434 cases and 770,180 controls;{ghodsian21} the UK Biobank GWAS by Fairfield et al (GCST90054782), which defined NAFLD from diagnostic codes recommended in consensus guidelines and included 4,761 cases and 373,227 controls;{fairfield22} and FinnGen release 5 (OpenGWAS record finn-b-NAFLD), which included 894 cases and 217,898 controls.{kurki23,elsworth20} We use the term NAFLD because the source studies defined cases under that name; the cases largely overlap with MASLD under the 2023 nomenclature,{rinella23} although the datasets did not apply its criteria. The three outcome datasets are not fully independent: the UK Biobank contributes to both the Ghodsian and Fairfield analyses, and FinnGen participants contribute to both the Ghodsian meta-analysis and FinnGen release 5. The exposure and two outcome datasets also draw on the UK Biobank, a point we address in the sample-overlap analysis (section 2.7).")
