D.h2('2.2. Selection of SNPs associated with remnant cholesterol')
D.para("We implemented a series of quality-control steps to ensure the reliability of the selected instruments. We began with a genome-wide significance threshold of P < 5 × 10^^−8^^, which gave 8,873 variants after removal of duplicated identifiers. To retain only independent instruments, we performed LD clumping with r^^2^^ < 0.001 within a 10,000 kb window, ranking variants by P value, using the 1000 Genomes Phase 3 European reference panel (503 individuals).{auton15} Variants absent from the reference panel (1,280 of 8,873) could not be clumped and were excluded; this is a limitation of the clumping step. This procedure yielded 52 independent variants (Supplemental Digital Content 2, Table S1). Where a target variant was unavailable in an outcome dataset, we searched for proxy variants (r^^2^^ ≥ 0.8 within 250 kb in the same reference panel) and aligned proxy alleles to the target by LD phase; proxies were used for 6 variants in the Ghodsian data and 3 in FinnGen, and 3 variants without a proxy (rs7534572, rs574454081 and rs532754706) were dropped from both.")
D.para("Instrument strength was evaluated with the F statistic, F = (β/SE)^^2^^, and with the variance explained (R^^2^^); we checked that every variant had F > 10,{burgess11} and additionally repeated the analysis restricted to variants with F ≥ 30. Variants with a minor allele frequency (MAF) < 0.01 were excluded in a further sensitivity analysis. During harmonisation, effects were aligned to the RC-increasing allele and ambiguous palindromic variants with intermediate allele frequency (0.42–0.58) were excluded to avoid strand-alignment errors (one variant, rs1293261, in every dataset). The final analyses used 48 variants (Ghodsian), 51 (Fairfield) and 48 (FinnGen). Finally, Steiger filtering was applied to assess directionality, which removes variants that explain more variance in the outcome than in the exposure.{hemani17} Because the APOE region (chromosome 19, 44.4–46.5 Mb) is strongly pleiotropic, we also repeated the analysis after excluding it. An earlier analysis of these data by our group used a different instrument set whose clumping settings were not recorded; we reconstructed it from its variant list (48 variants, including five in the APOE region) and report it as a sensitivity analysis.")
D.h2('2.3. Mendelian randomization analysis')
D.para("For the univariable MR analysis, we primarily used the IVW method within a multiplicative random-effects framework.{burgess13} Given that pleiotropy or invalid instruments can bias IVW estimates, we applied additional methods that make different assumptions about the instruments: MR-Egger regression,{bowden15} the weighted median estimator,{bowden16} and the simple and weighted mode-based estimators.{hartwig17} Effect estimates are reported as odds ratios (OR) of NAFLD per 1-SD higher genetically predicted RC with 95% confidence intervals (CI). Tests were two-sided with α = .05, and we did not adjust for multiple testing because a single exposure–outcome relation was examined in three datasets.")
D.h2('2.4. Heterogeneity, pleiotropy, outlier and influence diagnostics')
D.para("Heterogeneity among instruments was assessed with Cochran's Q statistic and the I^^2^^ index for IVW and with Rücker's Q for the MR-Egger framework,{bowden17} with P values above .05 suggesting low heterogeneity. The regression dilution of MR-Egger was assessed with I^^2^^~~GX~~, where values close to 100% indicate that the no-measurement-error (NOME) assumption is reasonable.{bowdenI2} Directional pleiotropy was assessed with the MR-Egger intercept and the MR-PRESSO global test.{verbanck18} Outlying variants were identified with MR-PRESSO (outlier test, 10,000 simulations) and radial MR,{bowden18} and influential variants with Cook's distance (threshold 4/n) and studentized residuals of the IVW regression; estimates were recalculated after removing MR-PRESSO outliers. Leave-one-out analyses determined whether any single variant drove the overall estimate, and funnel plots were inspected for asymmetry. The Steiger filter and the exclusion of the APOE region described above were treated as further sensitivity analyses. Variance explained in the outcome was approximated on the latent logistic scale without a prevalence assumption, which differs slightly from the TwoSampleMR implementation.")
D.h2('2.5. Extended estimators')
D.para("To test the consistency of the findings under a wider set of assumptions, we additionally applied maximum likelihood estimation, MR-Lasso,{rees19} the contamination mixture model,{conmix20} the debiased IVW estimator,{ye21} and robust adjusted profile score (MR-RAPS) with Tukey and Huber loss functions and overdispersion,{zhao20} as well as fixed-effect IVW, radial IVW, and IVW restricted to variants with F ≥ 30 or MAF ≥ 0.01, and re-estimated the random-effects IVW with the MendelianRandomization package as a software cross-check. These estimators relax, respectively, the assumptions of a balanced pleiotropy distribution, plurality validity, weak-instrument bias and measurement error in the exposure associations.{slob20} Together with the five methods above, they give 16 estimator variants per dataset, some of which (for example the software cross-check and the MAF restriction, which removed no variants) are re-estimations rather than independent methods.")
D.h2('2.6. Cross-dataset concordance')
D.para("To examine whether the three outcome datasets agree at the level of single variants, we calculated the Wald ratio (outcome effect divided by exposure effect) and its z score for each variant and compared them across datasets by Spearman rank correlation and by the proportion of variants with the same sign. Because the datasets share participants, concordance is expected to be higher than for independent replications and is reported descriptively.")
D.h2('2.7. Statistical power and sample overlap')
D.para("We calculated the minimum detectable OR with 80% power at α = .05 from the observed standard error of the IVW estimate in each dataset, and drew power curves across a range of true effects.{brion13,burgess14} Because the exposure GWAS and parts of the outcome data come from the UK Biobank, we considered the bias arising from sample overlap, which for a two-sample design is approximately the overlap proportion divided by the F statistic.{burgess16} We assumed complete overlap for the Fairfield dataset, which gives an upper bound, and note that the same applies in part to the Ghodsian data. Because variants were selected and estimated in the same exposure sample, the F statistic is also subject to winner's curse.{mounier23}")
D.h2('2.8. Positive control analysis')
D.para("To check that the instruments capture a known causal effect, we repeated the analysis with coronary artery disease (CAD) as the outcome, because RC is an established cause of ischaemic heart disease.{varbo13,nord16} We used the 1000 Genomes-based CARDIoGRAMplusC4D meta-analysis (GWAS Catalog GCST003116; 60,801 cases and 123,504 controls; mainly European, South Asian and East Asian ancestry),{nikpay15,sollis23} downloaded in its genome build 37 harmonised form and verified against the published MD5 checksum. The same 52 instruments were used. Instruments missing by rsID were replaced by a variant at the same chromosomal position with the same allele pair (two variants) or by the best proxy with r^^2^^ ≥ 0.8 in the European 1000 Genomes panel (two variants), and two variants without a usable proxy were dropped; one palindromic variant was removed. The analysis was run in Python with the same code as the NAFLD analysis and independently in R with the TwoSampleMR package, in which harmonisation and the five estimators were produced by the package and MR-PRESSO was run with the MRPRESSO package (10,000 simulations, seed 2026). The R analysis was run three times; the first two runs differed from the final run only in logging and in the random seed used for bootstrap standard errors, and all runs are provided. This analysis was added after the NAFLD analysis and was not pre-registered.")
D.h2('2.9. Software and validation')
D.para("The NAFLD analyses were conducted with custom Python code implementing the TwoSampleMR and MR-PRESSO algorithms,{hemani18,verbanck18} validated against R output of a previous analysis of the same data (it reproduced the IVW and MR-Egger estimates and standard errors, the Egger intercept, Cochran's Q, the MR-PRESSO global statistic and the outlier-corrected estimate). MR-PRESSO was re-run with the official R package, which identified the same outliers and gave the same corrected estimates as the Python implementation except for a negligible difference in the FinnGen P value (.250 in R against .24 in Python); the values reported here are the R results. Extended estimators were run in R 4.4.3 with the MendelianRandomization (version 0.10.0),{yavorska17} mr.raps (version 0.2) and RadialMR (version 1.2.1) packages. Figures were drawn with matplotlib. All code, console logs and intermediate files are available in the public repository listed under Code availability.")
# ---------- 3. Results ----------
D.h1('3. Results')
D.h2('3.1. Main results')
D.para("This analysis investigated a potential causal link between genetically predicted RC and NAFLD using Mendelian randomization. Following established criteria (genome-wide significance of P < 5 × 10^^−8^^ and clumping for independence), 52 SNPs associated with RC were selected as instrumental variables. After harmonisation, 48, 51 and 48 SNPs were retained for the Ghodsian, Fairfield and FinnGen datasets, respectively. The IVW analysis found no statistically significant association between RC and the risk of NAFLD in any dataset (Ghodsian: OR = "+est(G)+f"; Fairfield: OR = "+est(F)+"; FinnGen: OR = "+est(N)+"; Table 2 and Fig. 2). The weighted median, MR-Egger and mode-based estimators were likewise non-significant, although their point estimates differed in direction from the IVW estimate in some datasets (for example, MR-Egger OR = "+f"{g(G,'egger')['OR']:.3f} and {g(F,'egger')['OR']:.3f} in the Ghodsian and Fairfield data), a pattern consistent with imprecise estimates rather than a coherent effect.")
